RITE PARENT BODIES - IOPscience (https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0004-637X/809/1/6)\n  45. NATURE'S STARSHIPS. I. OBSERVED ABUNDANCES AND RELATIVE FREQUENCIES OF AMINO ACIDS IN METEORITES - IOPscience (https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0004-637X/783/2/140)\n  46. Hypothesis Article A Thermodynamic Basis for Prebiotic Amino Acid Synthesis (https://www.mso.anu.edu.au/~aditya/psi_seminars/2011/Pudritz/Higgs_and_Pudritz_2009.pdf)\n  47. Spectroscopy of Hydrothermal Reactions 25: Kinetics of the Decarboxylation of Protein Amino Acids and the Effect of Side Chains on Hydrothermal Stability (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231633952_Spectroscopy_of_Hydrothermal_Reactions_25_Kinetics_of_the_Decarboxylation_of_Protein_Amino_Acids_and_the_Effect_of_Side_Chains_on_Hydrothermal_Stability)\n  48. The reaction kinetics of alanine and glycine under hydrothermal conditions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/223355391_The_reaction_kinetics_of_alanine_and_glycine_under_hydrothermal_conditions)\n  49. Hydrothermal Reactions of Biomolecules Relevant for Microalgae Liquefaction (https://pubs.acs.org/iecred/article/54/47/11733/949086/Hydrothermal-Reactions-of-Biomolecules-Relevant)\n  50. Thermal Stability of Threonine in the Presence of a Marine Polyphenolic Material (https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.151.3707.214)\n  51. Radiolytic Decomposition and Radiation Sensitivity of Amino Acids in Aqueous Solution by Gamma Irradiation\\* (https://academic.oup.com/jrr/article/6/3-4/130/984246)\n  52. T- and pH-dependent OH radical reaction kinetics with glycine, alanine, serine, and threonine in the aqueous phase - Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics (RSC Publishing) (https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlelanding/2022/cp/d1cp05186e)\n  53. Primordial synthesis of amines and amino acids in a 1958 Miller H2S-rich spark discharge experiment (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1019191108)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Threonine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct a connected, module-level network with 26 molecule nodes, 21 reaction nodes, and 10 distinct source-and-processing pathways. These are not 10 independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses: the supplied evidence supports a partially demonstrated lactaldehyde-Strecker route, preparative glycine-acetaldehyde aldol chemistry, and trace discharge products. The pathways combine two acetaldehyde-supply modules with alternative nitrile hydrolysis or experimentally grounded glycine-supply modules. The discharge-to-aldehyde assignment and target-specific hydrolysis conditions are explicitly speculative. Threonine denotes the racemic threo pair, not selectively L-threonine; racemic allothreonine is tracked separately. Photochemical sulfide chemistry, acidic or alkaline hydrolysis, metal-mediated glycine synthesis, and Cu(II)-promoted aldol chemistry require separate reaction zones because their conditions are incompatible. No molecular oxygen is used. Hydrothermal reactions represent degradation only. Product accumulation requires cooling below approximately 323 K, shielding from continued irradiation, and removal from strongly catalytic degradation conditions. Organic formulas use neutral structural representations rather than every pH-dependent ionic form. S8 represents an idealized oxidized-sulfur endpoint, not an experimentally established quantitative sulfur balance. Reaction outputs identify accessible products, not yields or stereoisomer ratios. Pathway sequences are ordered module schedules; shared intermediates must be supplied in sufficient amounts for their respective branches.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h4H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H5NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactonitrile, stereochemistry unspecified\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactaldehyde, stereochemistry unspecified\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"threonine/allothreonine aminonitrile, unresolved stereoisomer pool\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H10N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"threoninamide/allothreoninamide, unresolved stereoisomer pool\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2RS,3SR)-2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"racemic threonine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2RS,3RS)-2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"racemic allothreonine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4N2/c3-1-2-4/h1,3H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(hydroxyimino)acetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylic acid oxime\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"zerovalent iron\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe2+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(2+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferrous ion\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe/q+2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"formula\": \"H+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"proton\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/p+1\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_024\",\n      \"formula\": \"S8\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cyclooctasulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"elemental sulfur\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_025\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_026\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Net photoreductive conversion of HCN to formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_024\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I) cyanide/cyanocuprate photoredox species\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I) cyanide/cyanocuprate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, UV-illuminated shallow water; 254 nm is the laboratory benchmark. Approximately neutral pH with phosphate buffering. Sulfide is a consumed electron donor, not merely a catalyst.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photochemical electron transfer reduces HCN to a methanimine-level intermediate; hydrolysis releases formaldehyde and ammonia. Oxidized sulfur is represented by an idealized S8 endpoint.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"HCN reductive homologation is supported by the cyanosulfidic framework of Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). This is a coarse-grained net module, not a claim of selective formaldehyde accumulation. Copper availability, sulfide concentration, and UV penetration constrain implementation; Ranjan et al. (2018) discuss sulfidic-anion supply limitations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 7-9; cyanide concentration and HCN retention must be sufficient. No irradiation is required for this addition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the formaldehyde carbonyl, followed by proton transfer to form glycolonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycolonitrile is a documented substrate and intermediate in the homologation chemistry of Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). The reaction is reversible and competes with other formaldehyde chemistry; no mineral catalyst is necessary.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolonitrile photoreduction to glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_024\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I) cyanide/cyanocuprate photoredox species\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I) cyanide/cyanocuprate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"254 nm laboratory irradiation; benchmark solution approximately 10 mM glycolonitrile, 30 mM sulfide, 33 mM phosphate at pH 7, with CuCN at approximately 10 mol%. Natural concentrations and illumination would differ.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoreduction of the nitrile group gives an imine-level intermediate whose hydrolysis produces glycolaldehyde and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This transformation is experimentally grounded in Ritson and Sutherland (2013). The benchmark does not establish that equivalent concentrations coexist naturally. Sulfide attenuation of UV and sequestration of copper remain important limitations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Net deoxygenative reduction of glycolaldehyde to acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_024\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu cyanide photoredox species\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu cyanide photoredox species\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic sulfide-bearing photochemical zone; approximately neutral pH. Conditions require tuning to limit further reduction, competing products, and acetaldehyde volatilization.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A net reductive deoxygenation replaces the hydroxyl group of glycolaldehyde with hydrogen, giving acetaldehyde. The equation compresses the underlying sulfur-containing and radical intermediates.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015), as summarized in the supplied research, place acetaldehyde production downstream of glycolaldehyde reduction. This node represents that net connection, not an independently established elementary mechanism or a transferable yield. Acetaldehyde supply and retention are major network bottlenecks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate reducing-discharge supply of small aldehydes and HCN\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K aqueous collector; transient discharge plasma is much hotter\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Electrical discharge through a locally reducing methane/ammonia/water-vapor mixture, followed by rapid aqueous capture. Nitrogen may be a background gas. No molecular oxygen is supplied; no global reducing atmosphere is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A coarse-grained radical network generates carbonyl compounds and nitriles; methyl/formyl coupling is a plausible contribution to acetaldehyde formation. The output list is a selected precursor pool, not an exhaustive discharge product inventory.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE PRECURSOR ASSIGNMENT. Ring et al. (1972) and Parker et al. (2011) support amino-acid production, including trace threonine, in particular reducing discharges. The supplied report does not establish the acetaldehyde flux or identify this as the mechanism of threonine formation. This chemically plausible alternative precursor source therefore requires direct aldehyde measurements and must not be treated as a demonstrated target pathway.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 7-9; rapid trapping helps retain volatile acetaldehyde. No enantioselective influence is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide addition to acetaldehyde followed by protonation forms lactonitrile, introducing an uncontrolled stereocenter.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Lactonitrile is the experimentally used precursor to lactaldehyde in Ritson and Sutherland (2013). Cyanohydrin formation is chemically justified without a mineral catalyst, but reversible dissociation and acetaldehyde self-condensation compete.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactonitrile photoreduction to lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_024\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I) cyanide/cyanocuprate photoredox species\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I) cyanide/cyanocuprate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"254 nm laboratory benchmark, sulfide electron donor, approximately neutral phosphate-buffered water. Target-specific optimization is required; concentrations from other nitrile experiments are not assumed to transfer unchanged.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reduction of the nitrile group to an imine followed by hydrolysis yields lactaldehyde while retaining the pre-existing, unresolved hydroxyl-bearing stereocenter.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson and Sutherland (2013) reported lactaldehyde in approximately 19% yield from lactonitrile. This is the strongest target-specific precursor evidence and a major low-flux bottleneck. Patel et al. (2015) incorporate the connection into their broader network; neither study establishes efficient complete threonine synthesis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactaldehyde Strecker aminonitrile formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia/ammonium and HCN/cyanide availability must overlap; approximately neutral to mildly alkaline conditions are candidate settings. Move out of continued intense irradiation after precursor generation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lactaldehyde forms an imine or iminium species with ammonia; cyanide addition gives 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile with uncontrolled alpha stereochemistry.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson and Sutherland (2013) reported conversion to the threonine/allothreonine aminonitrile, and Patel et al. (2015) discuss related aminonitrile formation. The supplied evidence provides neither an aminonitrile yield nor a threo:allo ratio. Both eventual diastereomer families are therefore retained without assigning equal yields.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate acid-catalyzed hydration of the threonine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous acid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Candidate acidic pool, approximately pH 1-2, separated from the cyanide-generating zone. Residence time and conversion are unknown; neutralize and cool promptly after processing.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Protonation of the nitrile enables water addition and tautomerization to the corresponding primary amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE TARGET-SPECIFIC EXTENSION. Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015) do not report completion of this hydrolysis for the threonine precursor. General nitrile chemistry supports the mechanism, but useful conversion within the stated surface temperature range is unestablished. This is a necessary experimental bottleneck, not a demonstrated yield-bearing step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate acidic amide hydrolysis to threonine and allothreonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous acid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 1-2; reaction time and recovery are unmeasured. Released nitrogen is mainly ammonium at this pH, represented by the network's ammonia node.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed acyl substitution converts the amide group to a carboxylic acid. Existing stereoisomers yield threonine and allothreonine unless epimerization or degradation intervenes.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE COMPLETION STEP. General amide hydrolysis makes this chemically credible, but the supplied target-specific studies stop at the aminonitrile. Neither free-amino-acid yield nor stereoisomer recovery is established (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013). Thermal decomposition and epimerization discussed by Schroeder and Bada (1977) can reduce recovery.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate alkaline hydration of the threonine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous base\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydroxide ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Candidate carbonate/hydroxide-rich alkaline pool, approximately pH 11-12. This is an alternative to acidic hydration, not an additional mandatory treatment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide addition to the nitrile followed by proton transfers gives the amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE TARGET-SPECIFIC EXTENSION. This is a chemically distinct hydrolysis condition from rxn_009, not a newly demonstrated prebiotic framework. Ritson and Sutherland (2013) provide the aminonitrile precursor but no alkaline hydrolysis result. Strong alkalinity may increase destructive side reactions, and attainable reaction rates remain unknown.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate alkaline amide hydrolysis to threonine and allothreonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous base\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydroxide-promoted hydrolysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 11-12, with sufficient alkalinity to support carboxylate formation. Organic products are represented by neutral formulas; subsequent neutralization and cooling are required.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide-mediated acyl substitution releases ammonia and produces amino-acid carboxylates. Protonation-state bookkeeping is omitted from the organic node identities.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE COMPLETION STEP. Alkaline amide cleavage is standard chemistry, but threonine recovery under these particular prebiotic conditions is unsupported by the supplied studies. Retro-aldol cleavage, epimerization, and other decomposition may outcompete accumulation; this must be tested rather than inferred from nitrile disappearance (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013; Schroeder and Bada, 1977).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated ammonia/ammonium and HCN/cyanide, approximately pH 7-9. Allocate a separate portion of the formaldehyde pool from that consumed in homologation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formaldehyde and ammonia form a methanimine-level species, which undergoes cyanide addition to aminoacetonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycine aminonitrile production is consistent with the experimentally grounded Strecker side chemistry described by Patel et al. (2015). This supplies the glycine branch without assuming glycine itself as a complex external feedstock; its competition with glycolonitrile formation is explicit.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoacetonitrile hydration to glycinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_026\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous acid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Candidate pH 1-2 processing zone, separated from HCN production. Residence time is not prescribed because surface-rate data are not supplied.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed nitrile hydration yields glycinamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The aminonitrile supply is supported by Patel et al. (2015); subdivision into hydration and amide cleavage follows standard Strecker chemistry. The particular temperature, pH, and residence-time window is a proposed implementation rather than a condition set validated by the supplied research.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycinamide hydrolysis to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous acid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydronium ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 1-2, followed by cooling and pH adjustment before aldol chemistry. Ammonium is represented by the ammonia node.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of the primary amide releases glycine and ammonium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the standard chemical completion of the glycine Strecker branch associated with the aminonitrile chemistry in Patel et al. (2015). The supplied report does not establish its kinetics in this proposed surface window. Glycine is less structurally vulnerable than the beta-hydroxylated target, but preparative hydrolysis yields must not be transferred automatically to natural pools.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of glyoxylate to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_018\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Nickel supported on silica-alumina, laboratory benchmark\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-303 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 8, anoxic, with ammonia/ammonium. Requires a confined hydrogen-bearing setting and a separate sulfide-poor reaction zone; an equivalent natural catalyst assemblage is not established.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glyoxylate and ammonia form an imine-level intermediate that is hydrogenated on nickel to glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) experimentally investigated glyoxylate and biological keto acids under metal-catalyzed H2/ammonium reductive-amination conditions. This evidence is used only for glycine, not extrapolated to direct threonine synthesis. The supported nickel catalyst is a laboratory material, and the availability of a comparably active natural surface remains unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate trapping as its oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Candidate mildly acidic to near-neutral solution. Hydroxylamine is an explicit external feedstock whose prebiotic supply is not solved by this network.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine condenses with the glyoxylate carbonyl to form an oxime and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska et al. (2019) obtained amino acids using hydroxylamine and zerovalent iron in an iron-promoted reaction network. The isolated oxime node is a mechanistic decomposition of that chemistry, not a claim that the supplied report separately measured this intermediate. Oxime formation is chemically well motivated, but hydroxylamine availability is an important feedstock limitation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-mediated oxime reduction to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_023\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reactive zerovalent iron surface in an anoxic, mildly acidic processing zone. Iron is consumed as a reductant; proton supply must be buffered or replenished. Oxide or sulfide passivation can suppress reaction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Electron transfer from Fe(0), protonation, C=N reduction, and N-O bond cleavage convert the oxime to glycine. Iron is oxidized to Fe(II); the net transformation requires four reducing equivalents per oxime.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska et al. (2019) support amino-acid formation using hydroxylamine and Fe(0). Representing the process as reduction of an explicit glyoxylate oxime is a mechanistic model rather than a separately validated elementary reaction. Fe(II) abundance alone cannot supply this reduction; access to Fe(0) is required.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional Cu(II)-glycinate aldol synthesis of threonine and allothreonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(II)-glycinate complexing promoter\", \"Aqueous base\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"313-333 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(II)-glycinate promoter; catalytic turnover is not assumed\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Candidate alkaline concentrated solution, approximately pH 9-11. Requires sufficiently high aldehyde activity and a sulfide-poor zone isolated from Fe(0). Product release from the copper complex is part of the net module. No oxygen-driven copper regeneration is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Copper chelation promotes alpha-carbon deprotonation of glycine. Carbon-carbon bond formation with acetaldehyde produces beta-hydroxy amino-acid complexes, followed by ligand exchange or decomplexation to release threonine and allothreonine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Sato, Okawa and Akabori (1957) reported up to approximately 64% combined threonine/allothreonine in preparative copper-glycinate chemistry. That yield is not assigned to these proposed natural conditions. High copper loading, base requirements, aldehyde volatility, reversibility, and maintenance of Cu(II) in an Fe(II)-rich anoxic setting are substantial obstacles. This is an experimentally demonstrated chemical benchmark with conditional, unproven geochemical implementation, not an established prebiotic route.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_020\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal retro-aldol loss of threonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"394-433 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-50 bar, sufficient to maintain liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Thermal-exposure sink, not a required synthesis stage. Actual rate and competing decomposition depend on pH, residence time, and dissolved metals.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cleavage of the alpha-beta carbon bond through retro-aldol chemistry gives glycine and acetaldehyde after proton transfer.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Threonine decomposition and epimerization are discussed by Schroeder and Bada (1977), while Aubrey, Cleaves and Bada (2009) emphasize amino-acid instability in hot vent fluids. The supplied research identifies glycine/acetaldehyde cleavage as an important loss channel. No extrapolated half-life is treated as a measured rate under these specific conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_021\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate hydrothermal retro-aldol loss of allothreonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"394-433 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-50 bar, sufficient to maintain liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Diastereomer-specific loss channel. Its rate is not assumed equal to that of threonine.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Retro-aldol cleavage of the beta-hydroxy amino-acid skeleton produces glycine and acetaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE DIASTEREOMER-SPECIFIC EXTENSION. The shared beta-hydroxy amino-acid skeleton supports this sink, and the supplied review reports decomposition or isomerization of allothreonine under aldol-processing conditions. Schroeder and Bada (1977) provide relevant decomposition context, but an independently quantified allothreonine rate under this hydrothermal window is not supplied.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical acetaldehyde supply followed by acidic Strecker completion\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN is homologated and reduced to acetaldehyde, which is converted through lactonitrile and lactaldehyde to the target aminonitrile. Acidic hydration and amide cleavage are proposed completion steps. Precursor chemistry is experimentally grounded; complete threonine production under this sequence is not demonstrated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical acetaldehyde supply followed by alkaline Strecker completion\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses the same experimentally grounded precursor assembly as P1 but branches through alkaline nitrile hydration and amide cleavage. This is a distinct processing path, not an independent demonstrated origin-of-life framework. Target recovery under alkaline conditions remains untested.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate discharge acetaldehyde supply followed by acidic Strecker completion\",\n      \"description\": \"A reducing-discharge precursor pool supplies acetaldehyde and HCN. The route then uses documented lactonitrile/lactaldehyde chemistry and proposed acidic hydrolysis. Both the quantitative discharge precursor assignment and target hydrolysis require validation; this does not assert the mechanism of historical discharge threonine formation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate discharge acetaldehyde supply followed by alkaline Strecker completion\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines the speculative discharge-derived acetaldehyde source with lactaldehyde Strecker chemistry and proposed alkaline hydrolysis. It differs from P3 in the hydrolysis branch; neither route has an established complete yield or stereoisomer ratio.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical aldehyde supply with Strecker-derived glycine and copper aldol coupling\",\n      \"description\": \"Separate portions of photochemically generated formaldehyde feed acetaldehyde production and glycine Strecker synthesis. After glycine hydrolysis, pH adjustment, and separation from sulfide, glycine and acetaldehyde enter the conditional Cu(II)-promoted aldol module. The full joined sequence is untested.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_026\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate discharge aldehyde supply with Strecker-derived glycine and copper aldol coupling\",\n      \"description\": \"The candidate discharge precursor module supplies formaldehyde, acetaldehyde, and HCN. Formaldehyde is converted to glycine through aminoacetonitrile and glycinamide before aldol coupling with retained acetaldehyde. Discharge precursor fluxes and the natural availability of the copper-promoted aldol conditions remain unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_026\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical acetaldehyde plus hydrogenative glycine synthesis and copper aldol coupling\",\n      \"description\": \"Photochemical homologation supplies acetaldehyde, while a separate nickel/H2 module converts supplied glyoxylate and ammonia to glycine. The streams converge only after leaving their incompatible catalyst environments. Glycine reductive amination and preparative aldol chemistry have experimental precedents, but their complete prebiotic integration does not.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate discharge acetaldehyde plus hydrogenative glycine synthesis and copper aldol coupling\",\n      \"description\": \"Candidate discharge-derived acetaldehyde is combined with glycine from nickel-catalyzed glyoxylate reductive amination. Hydrogen may be externally supplied; discharge hydrogen production does not establish the required 5 bar partial pressure. Final aldol synthesis retains the copper-availability and reversibility limitations.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical acetaldehyde plus oxime/iron-derived glycine and copper aldol coupling\",\n      \"description\": \"The acetaldehyde branch uses cyanosulfidic chemistry. A separate glyoxylate/hydroxylamine branch forms an oxime that is reduced by consumed Fe(0) to glycine. Remove residual iron and sulfide before conditional Cu(II)-mediated aldol coupling. Hydroxylamine supply and the compatibility of sequential processing remain unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate discharge acetaldehyde plus oxime/iron-derived glycine and copper aldol coupling\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines the alternative discharge precursor source with the hydroxylamine/Fe(0) glycine-supply branch, followed by copper-promoted aldol chemistry. This is a distinct combination of modules, not a reported complete synthesis. It depends on validating discharge acetaldehyde supply, obtaining hydroxylamine, and maintaining a separate Cu(II)-compatible reaction zone.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_016\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_013\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_016\",\n    \"mol_017\",\n    \"mol_024\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_020\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_020\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_020\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_019\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Threonine", "overall_strategy": "Construct a connected, module-level network with 26 molecule nodes, 21 reaction nodes, and 10 distinct source-and-processing pathways. These are not 10 independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses: the supplied evidence supports a partially demonstrated lactaldehyde-Strecker route, preparative glycine-acetaldehyde aldol chemistry, and trace discharge products. The pathways combine two acetaldehyde-supply modules with alternative nitrile hydrolysis or experimentally grounded glycine-supply modules. The discharge-to-aldehyde assignment and target-specific hydrolysis conditions are explicitly speculative. Threonine denotes the racemic threo pair, not selectively L-threonine; racemic allothreonine is tracked separately. Photochemical sulfide chemistry, acidic or alkaline hydrolysis, metal-mediated glycine synthesis, and Cu(II)-promoted aldol chemistry require separate reaction zones because their conditions are incompatible. No molecular oxygen is used. Hydrothermal reactions represent degradation only. Product accumulation requires cooling below approximately 323 K, shielding from continued irradiation, and removal from strongly catalytic degradation conditions. Organic formulas use neutral structural representations rather than every pH-dependent ionic form. S8 represents an idealized oxidized-sulfur endpoint, not an experimentally established quantitative sulfur balance. Reaction outputs identify accessible products, not yields or stereoisomer ratios. Pathway sequences are ordered module schedules; shared intermediates must be supplied in sufficient amounts for their respective branches.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C2H3NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyacetonitrile", "common_name": "glycolonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h4H,2H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "glycolaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C3H5NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanenitrile", "common_name": "lactonitrile, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H6O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanal", "common_name": "lactaldehyde, stereochemistry unspecified", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "threonine/allothreonine aminonitrile, unresolved stereoisomer pool", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C4H10N2O2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanamide", "common_name": "threoninamide/allothreoninamide, unresolved stereoisomer pool", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C4H9NO3", "iupac_name": "(2RS,3SR)-2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid", "common_name": "racemic threonine", "inchi": null, "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C4H9NO3", "iupac_name": "(2RS,3RS)-2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid", "common_name": "racemic allothreonine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C2H4N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetonitrile", "common_name": "aminoacetonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4N2/c3-1-2-4/h1,3H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "glyoxylic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "NH3O", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C2H3NO3", "iupac_name": "2-(hydroxyimino)acetic acid", "common_name": "glyoxylic acid oxime", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "iron", "common_name": "zerovalent iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "Fe2+", "iupac_name": "iron(2+)", "common_name": "ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "hydron", "common_name": "proton", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_024", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "cyclooctasulfur", "common_name": "elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_025", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_026", "formula": "C2H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetamide", "common_name": "glycinamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Net photoreductive conversion of HCN to formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_004", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003", "mol_024"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I) cyanide/cyanocuprate photoredox species"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Cu(I) cyanide/cyanocuprate", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, UV-illuminated shallow water; 254 nm is the laboratory benchmark. Approximately neutral pH with phosphate buffering. Sulfide is a consumed electron donor, not merely a catalyst."}, "mechanism": "Photochemical electron transfer reduces HCN to a methanimine-level intermediate; hydrolysis releases formaldehyde and ammonia. Oxidized sulfur is represented by an idealized S8 endpoint.", "reasoning": "HCN reductive homologation is supported by the cyanosulfidic framework of Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). This is a coarse-grained net module, not a claim of selective formaldehyde accumulation. Copper availability, sulfide concentration, and UV penetration constrain implementation; Ranjan et al. (2018) discuss sulfidic-anion supply limitations."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 7-9; cyanide concentration and HCN retention must be sufficient. No irradiation is required for this addition."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the formaldehyde carbonyl, followed by proton transfer to form glycolonitrile.", "reasoning": "Glycolonitrile is a documented substrate and intermediate in the homologation chemistry of Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). The reaction is reversible and competes with other formaldehyde chemistry; no mineral catalyst is necessary."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Glycolonitrile photoreduction to glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003", "mol_024"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I) cyanide/cyanocuprate photoredox species"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Cu(I) cyanide/cyanocuprate", "aqueous": true, "other": "254 nm laboratory irradiation; benchmark solution approximately 10 mM glycolonitrile, 30 mM sulfide, 33 mM phosphate at pH 7, with CuCN at approximately 10 mol%. Natural concentrations and illumination would differ."}, "mechanism": "Photoreduction of the nitrile group gives an imine-level intermediate whose hydrolysis produces glycolaldehyde and ammonia.", "reasoning": "This transformation is experimentally grounded in Ritson and Sutherland (2013). The benchmark does not establish that equivalent concentrations coexist naturally. Sulfide attenuation of UV and sequestration of copper remain important limitations."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Net deoxygenative reduction of glycolaldehyde to acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001", "mol_024"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu cyanide photoredox species"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Cu cyanide photoredox species", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic sulfide-bearing photochemical zone; approximately neutral pH. Conditions require tuning to limit further reduction, competing products, and acetaldehyde volatilization."}, "mechanism": "A net reductive deoxygenation replaces the hydroxyl group of glycolaldehyde with hydrogen, giving acetaldehyde. The equation compresses the underlying sulfur-containing and radical intermediates.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015), as summarized in the supplied research, place acetaldehyde production downstream of glycolaldehyde reduction. This node represents that net connection, not an independently established elementary mechanism or a transferable yield. Acetaldehyde supply and retention are major network bottlenecks."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Candidate reducing-discharge supply of small aldehydes and HCN", "inputs": ["mol_025", "mol_003", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_008", "mol_002", "mol_018"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K aqueous collector; transient discharge plasma is much hotter", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Electrical discharge through a locally reducing methane/ammonia/water-vapor mixture, followed by rapid aqueous capture. Nitrogen may be a background gas. No molecular oxygen is supplied; no global reducing atmosphere is assumed."}, "mechanism": "A coarse-grained radical network generates carbonyl compounds and nitriles; methyl/formyl coupling is a plausible contribution to acetaldehyde formation. The output list is a selected precursor pool, not an exhaustive discharge product inventory.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE PRECURSOR ASSIGNMENT. Ring et al. (1972) and Parker et al. (2011) support amino-acid production, including trace threonine, in particular reducing discharges. The supplied report does not establish the acetaldehyde flux or identify this as the mechanism of threonine formation. This chemically plausible alternative precursor source therefore requires direct aldehyde measurements and must not be treated as a demonstrated target pathway."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Acetaldehyde cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 7-9; rapid trapping helps retain volatile acetaldehyde. No enantioselective influence is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide addition to acetaldehyde followed by protonation forms lactonitrile, introducing an uncontrolled stereocenter.", "reasoning": "Lactonitrile is the experimentally used precursor to lactaldehyde in Ritson and Sutherland (2013). Cyanohydrin formation is chemically justified without a mineral catalyst, but reversible dissociation and acetaldehyde self-condensation compete."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Lactonitrile photoreduction to lactaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003", "mol_024"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I) cyanide/cyanocuprate photoredox species"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Cu(I) cyanide/cyanocuprate", "aqueous": true, "other": "254 nm laboratory benchmark, sulfide electron donor, approximately neutral phosphate-buffered water. Target-specific optimization is required; concentrations from other nitrile experiments are not assumed to transfer unchanged."}, "mechanism": "Reduction of the nitrile group to an imine followed by hydrolysis yields lactaldehyde while retaining the pre-existing, unresolved hydroxyl-bearing stereocenter.", "reasoning": "Ritson and Sutherland (2013) reported lactaldehyde in approximately 19% yield from lactonitrile. This is the strongest target-specific precursor evidence and a major low-flux bottleneck. Patel et al. (2015) incorporate the connection into their broader network; neither study establishes efficient complete threonine synthesis."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Lactaldehyde Strecker aminonitrile formation", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia/ammonium and HCN/cyanide availability must overlap; approximately neutral to mildly alkaline conditions are candidate settings. Move out of continued intense irradiation after precursor generation."}, "mechanism": "Lactaldehyde forms an imine or iminium species with ammonia; cyanide addition gives 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile with uncontrolled alpha stereochemistry.", "reasoning": "Ritson and Sutherland (2013) reported conversion to the threonine/allothreonine aminonitrile, and Patel et al. (2015) discuss related aminonitrile formation. The supplied evidence provides neither an aminonitrile yield nor a threo:allo ratio. Both eventual diastereomer families are therefore retained without assigning equal yields."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Candidate acid-catalyzed hydration of the threonine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Candidate acidic pool, approximately pH 1-2, separated from the cyanide-generating zone. Residence time and conversion are unknown; neutralize and cool promptly after processing."}, "mechanism": "Protonation of the nitrile enables water addition and tautomerization to the corresponding primary amide.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE TARGET-SPECIFIC EXTENSION. Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015) do not report completion of this hydrolysis for the threonine precursor. General nitrile chemistry supports the mechanism, but useful conversion within the stated surface temperature range is unestablished. This is a necessary experimental bottleneck, not a demonstrated yield-bearing step."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Candidate acidic amide hydrolysis to threonine and allothreonine", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 1-2; reaction time and recovery are unmeasured. Released nitrogen is mainly ammonium at this pH, represented by the network's ammonia node."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed acyl substitution converts the amide group to a carboxylic acid. Existing stereoisomers yield threonine and allothreonine unless epimerization or degradation intervenes.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE COMPLETION STEP. General amide hydrolysis makes this chemically credible, but the supplied target-specific studies stop at the aminonitrile. Neither free-amino-acid yield nor stereoisomer recovery is established (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013). Thermal decomposition and epimerization discussed by Schroeder and Bada (1977) can reduce recovery."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Candidate alkaline hydration of the threonine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous base"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "hydroxide ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Candidate carbonate/hydroxide-rich alkaline pool, approximately pH 11-12. This is an alternative to acidic hydration, not an additional mandatory treatment."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide addition to the nitrile followed by proton transfers gives the amide.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE TARGET-SPECIFIC EXTENSION. This is a chemically distinct hydrolysis condition from rxn_009, not a newly demonstrated prebiotic framework. Ritson and Sutherland (2013) provide the aminonitrile precursor but no alkaline hydrolysis result. Strong alkalinity may increase destructive side reactions, and attainable reaction rates remain unknown."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Candidate alkaline amide hydrolysis to threonine and allothreonine", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous base"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "hydroxide-promoted hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 11-12, with sufficient alkalinity to support carboxylate formation. Organic products are represented by neutral formulas; subsequent neutralization and cooling are required."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide-mediated acyl substitution releases ammonia and produces amino-acid carboxylates. Protonation-state bookkeeping is omitted from the organic node identities.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE COMPLETION STEP. Alkaline amide cleavage is standard chemistry, but threonine recovery under these particular prebiotic conditions is unsupported by the supplied studies. Retro-aldol cleavage, epimerization, and other decomposition may outcompete accumulation; this must be tested rather than inferred from nitrile disappearance (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013; Schroeder and Bada, 1977)."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Formaldehyde Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated ammonia/ammonium and HCN/cyanide, approximately pH 7-9. Allocate a separate portion of the formaldehyde pool from that consumed in homologation."}, "mechanism": "Formaldehyde and ammonia form a methanimine-level species, which undergoes cyanide addition to aminoacetonitrile.", "reasoning": "Glycine aminonitrile production is consistent with the experimentally grounded Strecker side chemistry described by Patel et al. (2015). This supplies the glycine branch without assuming glycine itself as a complex external feedstock; its competition with glycolonitrile formation is explicit."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Aminoacetonitrile hydration to glycinamide", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_026"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Candidate pH 1-2 processing zone, separated from HCN production. Residence time is not prescribed because surface-rate data are not supplied."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed nitrile hydration yields glycinamide.", "reasoning": "The aminonitrile supply is supported by Patel et al. (2015); subdivision into hydration and amide cleavage follows standard Strecker chemistry. The particular temperature, pH, and residence-time window is a proposed implementation rather than a condition set validated by the supplied research."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Glycinamide hydrolysis to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_026", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 1-2, followed by cooling and pH adjustment before aldol chemistry. Ammonium is represented by the ammonia node."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic cleavage of the primary amide releases glycine and ammonium.", "reasoning": "This is the standard chemical completion of the glycine Strecker branch associated with the aminonitrile chemistry in Patel et al. (2015). The supplied report does not establish its kinetics in this proposed surface window. Glycine is less structurally vulnerable than the beta-hydroxylated target, but preparative hydrolysis yields must not be transferred automatically to natural pools."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of glyoxylate to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_003", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Nickel supported on silica-alumina, laboratory benchmark"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-303 K", "pressure": "approximately 5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni/SiO2-Al2O3", "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 8, anoxic, with ammonia/ammonium. Requires a confined hydrogen-bearing setting and a separate sulfide-poor reaction zone; an equivalent natural catalyst assemblage is not established."}, "mechanism": "Glyoxylate and ammonia form an imine-level intermediate that is hydrogenated on nickel to glycine.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) experimentally investigated glyoxylate and biological keto acids under metal-catalyzed H2/ammonium reductive-amination conditions. This evidence is used only for glycine, not extrapolated to direct threonine synthesis. The supported nickel catalyst is a laboratory material, and the availability of a comparably active natural surface remains unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Glyoxylate trapping as its oxime", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_019"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-343 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Candidate mildly acidic to near-neutral solution. Hydroxylamine is an explicit external feedstock whose prebiotic supply is not solved by this network."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine condenses with the glyoxylate carbonyl to form an oxime and water.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019) obtained amino acids using hydroxylamine and zerovalent iron in an iron-promoted reaction network. The isolated oxime node is a mechanistic decomposition of that chemistry, not a claim that the supplied report separately measured this intermediate. Oxime formation is chemically well motivated, but hydroxylamine availability is an important feedstock limitation."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Iron-mediated oxime reduction to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_021", "mol_023"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_001", "mol_022"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-343 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reactive zerovalent iron surface in an anoxic, mildly acidic processing zone. Iron is consumed as a reductant; proton supply must be buffered or replenished. Oxide or sulfide passivation can suppress reaction."}, "mechanism": "Electron transfer from Fe(0), protonation, C=N reduction, and N-O bond cleavage convert the oxime to glycine. Iron is oxidized to Fe(II); the net transformation requires four reducing equivalents per oxime.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019) support amino-acid formation using hydroxylamine and Fe(0). Representing the process as reduction of an explicit glyoxylate oxime is a mechanistic model rather than a separately validated elementary reaction. Fe(II) abundance alone cannot supply this reduction; access to Fe(0) is required."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Conditional Cu(II)-glycinate aldol synthesis of threonine and allothreonine", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(II)-glycinate complexing promoter", "Aqueous base"], "conditions": {"temperature": "313-333 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Cu(II)-glycinate promoter; catalytic turnover is not assumed", "aqueous": true, "other": "Candidate alkaline concentrated solution, approximately pH 9-11. Requires sufficiently high aldehyde activity and a sulfide-poor zone isolated from Fe(0). Product release from the copper complex is part of the net module. No oxygen-driven copper regeneration is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Copper chelation promotes alpha-carbon deprotonation of glycine. Carbon-carbon bond formation with acetaldehyde produces beta-hydroxy amino-acid complexes, followed by ligand exchange or decomplexation to release threonine and allothreonine.", "reasoning": "Sato, Okawa and Akabori (1957) reported up to approximately 64% combined threonine/allothreonine in preparative copper-glycinate chemistry. That yield is not assigned to these proposed natural conditions. High copper loading, base requirements, aldehyde volatility, reversibility, and maintenance of Cu(II) in an Fe(II)-rich anoxic setting are substantial obstacles. This is an experimentally demonstrated chemical benchmark with conditional, unproven geochemical implementation, not an established prebiotic route."}, {"id": "rxn_020", "name": "Hydrothermal retro-aldol loss of threonine", "inputs": ["mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_008"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "394-433 K", "pressure": "10-50 bar, sufficient to maintain liquid water", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Thermal-exposure sink, not a required synthesis stage. Actual rate and competing decomposition depend on pH, residence time, and dissolved metals."}, "mechanism": "Cleavage of the alpha-beta carbon bond through retro-aldol chemistry gives glycine and acetaldehyde after proton transfer.", "reasoning": "Threonine decomposition and epimerization are discussed by Schroeder and Bada (1977), while Aubrey, Cleaves and Bada (2009) emphasize amino-acid instability in hot vent fluids. The supplied research identifies glycine/acetaldehyde cleavage as an important loss channel. No extrapolated half-life is treated as a measured rate under these specific conditions."}, {"id": "rxn_021", "name": "Candidate hydrothermal retro-aldol loss of allothreonine", "inputs": ["mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_008"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "394-433 K", "pressure": "10-50 bar, sufficient to maintain liquid water", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Diastereomer-specific loss channel. Its rate is not assumed equal to that of threonine."}, "mechanism": "Retro-aldol cleavage of the beta-hydroxy amino-acid skeleton produces glycine and acetaldehyde.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE DIASTEREOMER-SPECIFIC EXTENSION. The shared beta-hydroxy amino-acid skeleton supports this sink, and the supplied review reports decomposition or isomerization of allothreonine under aldol-processing conditions. Schroeder and Bada (1977) provide relevant decomposition context, but an independently quantified allothreonine rate under this hydrothermal window is not supplied."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Photochemical acetaldehyde supply followed by acidic Strecker completion", "description": "HCN is homologated and reduced to acetaldehyde, which is converted through lactonitrile and lactaldehyde to the target aminonitrile. Acidic hydration and amide cleavage are proposed completion steps. Precursor chemistry is experimentally grounded; complete threonine production under this sequence is not demonstrated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Photochemical acetaldehyde supply followed by alkaline Strecker completion", "description": "Uses the same experimentally grounded precursor assembly as P1 but branches through alkaline nitrile hydration and amide cleavage. This is a distinct processing path, not an independent demonstrated origin-of-life framework. Target recovery under alkaline conditions remains