12 reactions are balanced as written, nine are balanceable with coefficients, and none violates conservation. Coefficients associated with S8 or the two stereoisomer outputs are not evidence of implausible elementary molecularity; these are net modules. Correct the mol_022 formula/InChI inconsistency without reclassifying rxn_018 as unbalanced. Racemic threonine within a threo/allo mixture satisfies the declared target, so lack of L-selectivity is not penalized. Literature alignment is good at the strategy level: the network distinguishes demonstrated precursor chemistry, speculative target-specific hydrolysis, preparative aldol chemistry and degradation. However, individual reaction records lack source-to-step citations and experimental condition sets. The decisive limitations are unvalidated hydrolysis completion, conditional Cu-glycinate operation and product release, uncertain discharge precursor delivery, and missing physical transfer and catalyst-speciation details. Declaring separate zones and cool, shielded storage is appropriate but does not demonstrate that their output mixtures can be connected successfully. Sulfur speciation and intermediate retention also remain unquantified. rxn_020 is a credible degradation sink; rxn_021 is a plausible analogue with less direct support. Neither is counted as target synthesis. The pathway count and shared intermediates provide no independent evidentiary bonus. P5 receives conditional pathway-level approval under the requested 0.8 threshold, but the complete network is not approved: overall confidence remains below 0.9.\"\n}", "parsed_response": {"pathway_evaluations": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.85, "rxn_002": 0.94, "rxn_003": 0.89, "rxn_004": 0.82, "rxn_006": 0.94, "rxn_007": 0.9, "rxn_008": 0.9, "rxn_009": 0.77, "rxn_010": 0.72}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.85, "pathway_coherence": 0.88, "environmental_progression": 0.65, "target_achievement": 0.75, "overall_confidence": 0.78, "feedback": "The carbon-skeleton progression is coherent and consistent with the photochemical modules described by Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). Lactonitrile photoreduction and lactaldehyde Strecker chemistry have direct support. Acidic completion is chemically plausible, but the supplied research does not establish target-specific hydrolysis conditions or recovered threonine yield. Separate processing zones are appropriately acknowledged, but their conditions and transfer mechanisms are not specified.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "suggestions": ["Attach primary-source protocols to the photochemical steps, including copper speciation, loading, wavelength, pH, concentrations and irradiation time.", "Measure aminonitrile-to-amide-to-amino-acid conversion under specified acidic conditions, including competing degradation and threo/allo recovery.", "Specify how intermediates leave the irradiated sulfidic zone, undergo hydrolysis and reach protected storage without excessive losses."]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.85, "rxn_002": 0.94, "rxn_003": 0.89, "rxn_004": 0.82, "rxn_006": 0.94, "rxn_007": 0.9, "rxn_008": 0.9, "rxn_011": 0.73, "rxn_012": 0.68}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.83, "pathway_coherence": 0.87, "environmental_progression": 0.64, "target_achievement": 0.72, "overall_confidence": 0.77, "feedback": "The supported precursor sequence reaches the correct aminonitrile pool. Alkaline nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis are reasonable transformations, not conservation violations, but their effectiveness for this substrate pool remains unestablished. Prolonged alkaline treatment could alter stereoisomer distributions or increase competing decomposition. Without specified pH, temperature and residence time, this branch cannot be treated as experimentally equivalent to successful amino-acid production.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "suggestions": ["Esta