m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Threonine, represented as racemic threonine with allothreonine tracked separately\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct an evidence-qualified network around lactaldehyde Strecker chemistry and glycine\u2013acetaldehyde aldol chemistry, with two additional discharge-derived product inventories. The ten pathways are distinct graph routes: two Strecker hydrolysis variants, six combinations of glycine supply and copper-complex release, and two experimentally different discharge systems. They are not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic mechanisms. The supplied literature does not establish an integrated, selective prebiotic synthesis of free threonine. Acetaldehyde is both an externally supplied simple feedstock and a photochemical product; the aldol pathways explicitly use the external feedstock rather than assuming that a sulfide/cyanide-containing photolysate can enter Cu(II) chemistry. Its concentrated delivery remains unresolved. Target-specific aminonitrile hydrolysis, compatibility of upstream glycine mixtures with copper chemistry, and environmental copper availability remain conditional. Reported experimental conditions are distinguished from proposed screening conditions in individual records. Acidic copper release is followed by dilution int