tions\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.86,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.7,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.68,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.88\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.79,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.84,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.65,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.66,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.73,\n      \"feedback\": \"The precursor sequence is consistent with Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). The reported 19% yield concerns lactaldehyde, not free threonine. Acidic aminonitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis are chemically plausible, but target-specific conversion and product survival are not established by the supplied evidence. Weakly acidic recovery can give net-neutral, predominantly zwitterionic threonine at an appropriate pH. The submitted reaction entries do not provide the detailed catalyst, irradiation, hydrolysis and transfer conditions needed to validate environmental compatibility.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Attach reaction-specific CuCN loading, UV spectrum and exposure, pH, temperature and concentration records, distinguishing reported conditions from proposals.\",\n        \"Measure aminonitrile-to-amide-to-amino-acid conversion and competing degradation during acidic hydrolysis.\",\n        \"Demonstrate transfer from the photochemical mixture to hydrolysis and quantify free threonine and allothreonine separately.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.86,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.64,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.61,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.92\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.74,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.82,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.62,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.61,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.69,\n      \"feedback\": \"The precursor chemistry has the same literature support as P1, but alkaline completion remains insufficiently demonstrated for this beta-hydroxy aminonitrile pool. Conditions that accelerate nitrile and amide hydrolysis may also promote cleavage, epimerization or other degradation. Epimerization alone does not invalidate a racemic target, but it must be distinguished from destruction and redistribution between threonine and allothreonine. Protonation and cooling are valid final operations if the amino acids survive the preceding treatment.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Establish a pH-temperature-time window in which hydrolysis outpaces product degradation.\",\n        \"Quantify residual aminonitrile, amide, both amino-acid diastereomer pools and cleavage products.\",\n        \"Specify the alkaline resource, subsequent proton source, dilution and cooling conditions.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.87,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_020\": 0.78,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.88\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.83,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.84,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.63,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.76,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.76,\n      \"feedback\": \"Strecker-derived glycine and copper-glycinate aldol chemistry provide a credible designed route. Sato, Okawa and Akabori (1957) support preparative threonine/allothreonine formation, but not operation with an untreated upstream Strecker mixture under geochemical concentrations. Acid dissociation followed by dilution can produce a free amino-acid fraction and is correctly not described as copper-free isolation. Nevertheless, copper rebinding and the final free-product fraction depend on pH, dilution and competing ligands.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_019\",\n        \"rxn_020\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Test copper complexation and aldol conversion using the actual glycine hydrolysate, including residual cyanide, ammonium and salts.\",\n        \"Specify acetaldehyde concentration, delivery and retention, base loading and copper speciation.\",\n        \"Measure free versus copper-bound threonine after acidification and the proposed dilution rather than inferring release from pH alone.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.87,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_021\": 0.89\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.87,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.87,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.66,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.84,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.81,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is a relatively strong designed branch: glycine synthesis is conventional, the copper-mediated aldol has preparative support, and copper sulfide precipitation supplies a credible ligand-release mechanism. The network correctly treats copper as consumed during sulfide release rather than regenerated catalytically. Remaining limitations concern upstream-mixture compatibility, environmentally supportable copper and acetaldehyde concentrations, and recovery from the precipitating mixture. These are integration uncertainties, not conservation failures.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_019\",\n        \"rxn_021\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Demonstrate aldol formation from the actual Strecker-derived glycine mixture using externally supplied acetaldehyde.\",\n        \"Introduce sulfide only after aldol formation and measure dissolved copper, precipitated copper and amino-acid recovery.\",\n        \"Check adsorption or coprecipitation losses and distinguish combined threonine/allothreonine yield from threonine yield.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_020\": 0.78,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.88\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.83,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.81,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.6,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.75,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.74,\n      \"feedback\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) support metal-catalysed glyoxylate reductive amination as a glycine supply module; this does not constitute direct reductive-amination evidence for threonine, and the network appropriately avoids that claim. The handoff from a nickel/hydrogen system to Cu(II) chemistry is not operationally specified. The 'Biochemical' label does not identify a physical environment or establish availability of the laboratory catalyst and hydrogen pressure. Final free-product abundance remains conditional on copper speciation after acid release and dilution.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_019\",\n        \"rxn_020\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify the nickel catalyst and distinguish the reported approximately 5 bar H2, 22 degrees C and pH 8 conditions from any proposed environmental substitute.\",\n        \"Define catalyst separation and hydrogen-pressure release before copper complexation and acetaldehyde addition.\",\n        \"Measure glycine-mixture compatibility and the free threonine fraction after acidic release and dilution.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_021\": 0.89\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.86,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.84,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.61,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.82,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.78,\n      \"feedback\": \"The individual glycine-supply and aldol modules have experimental analogues, and sulfide precipitation provides a stronger copper-removal mechanism than dilution alone. However, successful operation with the nickel-derived mixture has not been established. Residual catalyst and reducing conditions must be managed before introducing Cu(II) and acetaldehyde; their compatibility cannot be inferred simply because glycine is present. Sulfide release is chemically plausible but requires a measured recovery balance.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_019\",\n        \"rxn_021\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Demonstrate the nickel-to-copper handoff with measurements of residual metal, reductant and competing ligands.\",\n        \"Document the physical setting supporting hydrogen pressure and subsequent transfer to the aldol environment.\",\n        \"Quantify free amino acids, residual dissolved copper and product losses during sulfide precipitation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_020\": 0.78,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.88\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.82,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.81,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.6,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.74,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.74,\n      \"feedback\": \"Oxime formation followed by iron-mediated reduction is a plausible glycine supply route, consistent in general with Muchowska et al. (2019). That evidence does not independently validate every isolated intermediate or the subsequent copper handoff. Residual Fe(0) can consume Cu(II), and residual hydroxylamine can alter its oxidation state. These are specific compatibility problems requiring control. Acid release and dilution then introduce the same unresolved free-versus-bound product question as P3.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_017\",\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_019\",\n        \"rxn_020\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Verify oxime conversion and glycine formation under the proposed iron loading, acidity and residence time.\",\n        \"Remove residual Fe(0) and quantify hydroxylamine and dissolved iron before Cu(II) addition.\",\n        \"Demonstrate aldol conversion and free threonine recovery from this specific upstream mixture.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_021\": 0.89\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.85,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.83,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.61,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.81,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.77,\n      \"feedback\": \"This branch combines plausible oxime/iron glycine synthesis with experimentally supported copper aldol chemistry and a credible precipitation-driven release. Its principal weakness is compatibility between the iron/hydroxylamine mixture and Cu(II), not the identity of the glycin