 general with Muchowska et al. (2019). That evidence does not independently validate every isolated intermediate or the subsequent copper handoff. Residual Fe(0) can consume Cu(II), and residual hydroxylamine can alter its oxidation state. These are specific compatibility problems requiring control. Acid release and dilution then introduce the same unresolved free-versus-bound product question as P3.', 'This branch combines plausible oxime/iron glycine synthesis with experimentally supported copper aldol chemistry and a credible precipitation-driven release. Its principal weakness is compatibility between the iron/hydroxylamine mixture and Cu(II), not the identity of the glycine feedstock. Downstream sulfide can also interact with carried-over metals, affecting precipitation, reagent demand and product retention. The network acknowledges conditional integration but does not supply measurements that resolve it.', 'Parker et al. (2011) support threonine detection in the archived H2S-rich discharge experiment. The supplied findings do not establish a separately quantified allothreonine inventory for this particular experiment. Archival contamination, free-versus-hydrolysed material and electrode effects require qualification, but do not justify dismissing the reported detection. The balanced equation is valid bookkeeping for an inventory module, not a demonstrated exclusive conversion into the listed products; S8 is appropriately identified as representative rather than exclusive.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Threonine, represented as racemic threo-threonine rather than selectively L-threonine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This evidence-qualified network contains 40 molecule nodes, 30 reaction nodes and 10 distinct reaction schedules. These are not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses: P1-P2 are cyanosulfidic/Strecker routes with unvalidated target-specific hydrolysis; P3-P8 combine three glycine sources with preparative copper-glycinate aldol chemistry and two different product-release sequences; P9-P10 represent experimentally observed discharge inventories with explicitly conditional environmental recovery. Formaldehyde, acetaldehyde and glyoxylic acid are permissible small-organic boundary feedstocks. Their external supply and concentration are not claimed to have been demonstrated by the discharge experiments. Consequently, P3-P8 do not silently transfer sulfide/cyanide-containing photolysates into copper(II) chemistry. Internally generated acetaldehyde is an additional supply possibility, but its purification for the aldol branch remains unresolved. Reported experimental conditions are distinguished from proposed screening conditions in individual reactions. The network does not demonstrate integrated throughput, free-product yield or geochemical availability of concentrated copper(II), hydroxylamine, acid or alkalinity. Copper removal, iron removal, volatile retention and precursor survival are explicit handoff gates, not assumed quantitative operations. Threonine and allothreonine are tracked separately; neither enantioselection nor a fixed diastereomer ratio is asserted. Neutral parent formulas describe constitutional intermediates whose aqueous protonation equilibria are implicit; explicit salt nodes represent chemically important workup states. Salts are electrically neutral formula units, avoiding ambiguous ionic formulas. Unresolved stereochemical or coordination mixtures have null InChI values. The schema's intermediate role also covers coproducts and precipitated sinks. Sulfur is represented by S8 as a formal zero-valent endpoint, although actual photolysates can contain polysulfides and metal sulfides. Inferred coefficients describe atom-balanceable net modules, not yields, stereoisomer ratios or elementary molecularity. Product accumula