nd conditioning of residual ammonia before copper-glycinate formation.\",\n        \"Measure integrated glyoxylate-to-free-threonine recovery through the aldol and copper-removal operations.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_023\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_024\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_027\": 0.87\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.84,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.85,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.58,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.77,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.76,\n      \"feedback\": \"The chemical strategy is sound in outline: demonstrated reductive-amination chemistry supplies glycine, copper activation enables aldol addition, and sulfide can release the amino-acid ligands. Confidence is limited by the unspecified nickel/hydrogen environment, the untested composition of the transferred glycine solution and target recovery during direct decomplexation. The concern is compatibility and evidence for the linked operations, not the use of different environments or the number of steps.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_019\",\n        \"rxn_024\",\n        \"rxn_027\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Run the aldol reaction on a characterized, conditioned nickel-amination product solution and compare it with purified glycine.\",\n        \"Quantify free threonine and residual copper after sulfide treatment under a specified pH and separation protocol.\",\n        \"Provide a physical basis for the adopted hydrogen pressure and catalyst availability.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_020\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_021\": 0.73,\n        \"rxn_022\": 0.83,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_023\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_024\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_025\": 0.93,\n        \"rxn_026\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.97,\n     